{"name":"Bird Flower","hex":"#d0c117","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bird","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An animal of the clade (traditionally class) Aves in the phylum Chordata, characterized by being warm-blooded, having feathers and wings usually capable of flight, having a beaked mouth, and laying eggs.","Any winged animal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To observe or identify wild birds in their natural environment.","To catch or shoot birds; to hunt birds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bird"},{"term":"flower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colorful, conspicuous structure associated with angiosperms, frequently scented and attracting various insects, and which may or may not be used for sexual reproduction.","A reproductive structure in angiosperms (flowering plants), often conspicuously colourful and typically including sepals, petals, and either or both stamens and/or a pistil."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put forth blooms.","To decorate with pictures of flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bird","blocks":[{"text":"Birds are vertebrate animals constituting the class Aves, characterised by feathers, toothless beaked jaws, the laying of hard-shelled eggs, a high metabolic rate, a four-chambered heart, and a strong yet lightweight skeleton. They are warm-blooded, live worldwide, and range in size from the 5.5 cm (2.2 in) bee hummingbird to the 2.8 m common ostrich. There are over 11,000 living species of birds, classified in 44 orders. More than half of the world's bird species are passerine or \"perching\" birds. Nearly all birds have wings whose development varies according to the life style of each species. Wings, which are modified forelimbs, gave birds the ability to fly, although further evolution has led to the loss of flight in some birds, including ratites, penguins, and diverse endemic island species. The only known groups without wings are the extinct moa and elephant birds. Birds are the only extant members of Dinosauria, and are a type of paravian theropods. The digestive and respiratory systems of birds are uniquely adapted for flight. Some bird species of aquatic environments, particularly seabirds and some waterbirds, have further evolved for swimming. The study of birds is called ornithology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kleiber_Flug.jpg/330px-Kleiber_Flug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg/500px-Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg/1280px-Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg/500px-Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Archaeopteryx is often considered the oldest known true bird.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArchaeopteryx_lithographica_(Berlin_specimen).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Birds_and_dinosaurs.webp/500px-Birds_and_dinosaurs.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Birds_and_dinosaurs.webp/500px-Birds_and_dinosaurs.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Simplified phylogenetic tree showing the relationship between modern birds and other dinosaurs[21]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABirds_and_dinosaurs.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Anchiornis_feathers.jpg/500px-Anchiornis_feathers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Anchiornis_feathers.jpg/1280px-Anchiornis_feathers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Anchiornis_feathers.jpg/500px-Anchiornis_feathers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anchiornis huxleyi is an important source of information on the early evolution of birds in the Late Jurassic period.[27]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnchiornis_feathers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Confuciusornis_male.jpg/500px-Confuciusornis_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Confuciusornis_male.jpg/1280px-Confuciuso